search\nResearch Area\nCold atoms, Optics and Quantum Information\nCondensed Matter Experiments and Advanced Materials\nCondensed Matter Theory, Statistical and Computational Physics\nParticle Physics and Cosmology\nSoft Matter and Biological Physics\nWave Functional Materials and Physics\nPublications\nResearch Institutes and Centers\nList of Research Facilities\nDonations\nCommunity Outreach\nThe Center for the Development of the Gifted and Talented\nHong Kong Physics Olympiad\nMini-lectures in science for secondary schools\nNews & Seminars\nNews\nSeminars\nMenu\nParticle physics and cosmology\nSections\nLeft Column\nMain navigation\nResearch Area\nCold atoms, Optics and Quantum Information\nCondensed Matter Experiments and Advanced Materials\nCondensed Matter Theory, Statistical and Computational Physics\nParticle Physics and Cosmology\nSoft Matter and Biological Physics\nWave Functional Materials and Physics\nPublications\nResearch Institutes and Centers\nList of Research Facilities\nDonations\nMiddle Column\nText Area\nParticle physics and cosmology aim to answer fundamental scientific questions about nature. How did the Universe begin and evolve? What are the basic components of matter, and how do they interact? And how will these components influence the future of the Universe? While these questions are addressed at different physical scales, from subatomic particles to the entire Universe, they could be theoretically interconnected. Advancing our understanding of these puzzles and uncovering their solutions requires strong support from experiments and observations. Research at HKUST encompasses many aspects of particle physics and cosmology, such as dark matter theory and new physics, cosmological collider, collider and neutrino experiments, cosmological observations, etc..\nFor more information, please visit the HKUST Center for Fundamental Physics\nRelated Information\nProf. Tao Liu\u2019s group\nProf. Kam-biu Luk's group\nProf. Kirill Prokofiev\u2019s gro